 "rulebook": "FIBA Official Basketball Rules 2024: Art. 2 (court), Art. 3 and the Basketball Equipment appendix (backboard, ring, size-7 ball), Art. 13 (how the ball is played), Art. 16 (goal), Art. 25 (travelling)",
 "deviations": [
  "Art. 3 (equipment): the hand holds the ball by a grip switched on and off, not by fingers: the grip takes the ball when its surface is within 3.5 cm of the palm and it moves at most 6 m/s relative to the hand; the ball is released when the grip is switched off.",
  "Basketball Equipment (basket): the ring is rigid (no pressure release) and the net is only drawn: it does not slow the ball. A goal is judged at the ring: the ball's centre down through the ring's plane and on to one ball diameter below it.",
  "The pass is thrown by a machine, not a team-mate: always the same chest pass, at a fixed time.",
  "Art. 13: a touch of the ball by a leg or a foot is always taken as deliberate (a violation); intent cannot be judged.",
  "Art. 25.2.1: a ball caught with a foot off the floor is judged by the two-step rule without the pivoting it allows after a stop: after two steps no foot may touch the floor again while the ball is held.",
  "One attempt, no opponent, no rebound: a pass that is not caught on the fly, and touching the ball after the shot's release, end the attempt."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Catch and shoot: catch the chest pass that comes to you from the left wing, then shoot from where you stand so that the ball enters the basket from above.",
  "rules": "The official basket: a rigid ring of 0.45 m inner diameter (0.018 m metal), its top edge 3.05 m above the floor, its centre 0.375 m in front of a backboard 1.8 m wide and 1.05 m high whose lower edge is 2.9 m above the floor. The ball is a size-7 basketball (0.239 m across, 0.62 kg, it keeps 0.85 of its speed in a bounce on the floor); it flies in air (drag and spin lift). It is passed to you 2.0 s after the start: a chest pass of 8.0 m/s from 5.0 m away. In order: you catch the ball (a hand's grip takes it) before it has touched the floor; you let go of it (the shot); its centre goes down through the ring's plane inside the ring and on down to one ball diameter below it, which is the success. Travelling, from the catch to the shot's release: if both your feet are on the floor at the catch, the first foot you lift makes the other your pivot foot; the pivot foot, and before a foot is lifted either foot, may not slide more than 0.2 m along the floor (in the steps case below: any foot that is on the floor, from where it stood or was put down), and once it has left the floor no foot may touch the floor again before the ball has left your hand. If a foot is off the floor at the catch, you may take at most 2 steps (a step: a foot touching the floor after it has been off it for at least 0.03 s; both feet landing within 0.03 s of each other are one step; not the same foot twice in a row), and after them no foot may touch the floor again before the release. You fail if anything of you touches the ball before it is passed; if the passed ball touches the floor, or one of your legs or feet, before you have caught it; if you travel; if any part of you touches the ball more than 0.05 s after the shot's release (it cannot be gripped again); if the ball touches the floor before it is through the ring; if the ball's centre comes up through the ring from below; if you fall before the ball is through (a fall: your pelvis lower than 0.35 m above the floor, or any part of you other than your feet on the floor); or after 15 s.",
  "venue": "A FIBA court. You stand at the top of the key facing the basket (+x): the point under the ring's centre is 5.8 m ahead of your pelvis (inside the three-point line; the free-throw line is 1.575 m ahead of you). The passer stands on the left wing: the ball waits 1.05 m ahead of you and 4.96 m to your left, 1.25 m above the floor. The pass is aimed at the point 0.4 m straight ahead of where your pelvis starts, 1.25 m above the floor: it leaves on an upward arc (28 degrees) and is there 0.76 s later, moving at about 7.2 m/s to your right and downwards. A hand's closed grip takes the ball when the ball's surface comes within 3.5 cm of the palm and the ball moves at most 6 m/s relative to the hand; a ball is released when the grip is switched off.",
  "difficulty": "The pass arrives faster than the grip can take it: the hand has to be on the ball's path and moving with it. Then a 0.62 kg ball must leave that hand at about 9 m/s on a steep arc towards a ring 5.8 m away, from a body that may not take a step. The built-in walking gait steps on the spot even at a zero command, so while the ball is in your hand its steps are counted like any others."
 },
